#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""aeon-modelshare — fleet-free Model Share discovery over IPFS.

Every Orb gossips its shared-model catalog on a well-known libp2p pubsub topic
and merges every other Orb's announcement into a local registry, so any Orb on
the IPFS network converges on the SAME global model index with NO shared token,
NO fleet enrollment, and no central server. The supervisor reads the registry
for the Model Share console; this daemon just keeps it fresh.

Loops (threads), all driving the `aeon-ipfs` infra script so the kubo CLI runs
as the ipfs service user:
  * SUB: `aeon-ipfs sub-json <topic>` streams peer announcements, each wrapped
    in an envelope naming the peer libp2p authenticated as its sender. An
    announcement is merged only when the peer id it claims IS that sender —
    otherwise any node on the topic could rewrite any Orb's catalog.
  * PUB: every ANNOUNCE_EVERY seconds (or immediately when the supervisor
    touches <run>/modelshare-announce after a share/unshare) publish this
    Orb's {peer_id, label, addrs, models[]} to the topic.
  * DHT: periodically `routing findprovs` a well-known rendezvous CID that
    every Magick Orb pins. That returns live Orb PeerIDs on the Amino DHT; the
    ones we are not already connected to are dialed (direct, hole-punch, or
    circuit-relay) so floodsub actually has a mesh. Without this, two NATed
    strangers both "on IPFS" never see each other's catalogs.
  * DIAL: a small fixed pool of workers does all connecting. Peers we are
    already connected to are skipped and failures back off, so the cost of
    staying meshed does not grow with every announcement heard.

The announcement is plain JSON. It carries only public content (CIDs +
already-public model-card metadata); the weights themselves move over the
normal IPFS swarm when a peer pins the CID.
"""
import base64
import hashlib
import json
import os
import queue
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import unicodedata

